n, so `noDelay` is set and the 5 s wait (savetimeoutdelay) is skipped.\n- The browser never sends that message when a tab closes. The socket.io client closes the connection on `beforeunload`, and the server only sees `transport close`. A page reload looks exactly the same, and reloads are what the 5 s wait protects (preserve clause #1).\n- Saving immediately on `transport close` would therefore break the contract. An explicit close only exists when the integrator calls `asc_coAuthoringDisconnect`.\n\n**Follow-up:** I recorded a request in `.theseus/spawn/` to measure a close driven by the integrator (via `asc_coAuthoringDisconnect`), which should get the ~0.85 s save.\n\n**Acceptance:**\n- **`.theseus/oracle/unit.sh`:** passes (178 passed, 1 skipped). The previous failure was in timing-sensitive timeout tests in `tests/unit/request.tests.js` (`timeout of 300ms exceeded`, `aborted`). This packet touches none of that code, and the same tests pass on the rerun.\n- **`bash .theseus/oracle/journeys/save.sh`:** fails before measuring anything. `serve-integrated.sh` line 29 runs `cd external/sdkjs/build`, and `external/` doesn't exist in this worktree or in the main checkout. The factory has to provide that checkout; fixing it is outside my target. Since no code changed, the journey time wouldn't move anyway.\n\nThe Executor log now covers both attempts. My friction line for this packet is in `friction.jsonl`, `theseus scope-check` reports scope ok, and both commits are on `pkt/PKT-0137`.\n\n**Lessons for the Atlas:**\n- Every browser tab close or reload reaches the server as `transport close`, so it always waits the 5 s before saving.\n- web-apps sends nothing to the server on unload. An explicit close is only possible through the API.","ttft_ms":3259,"type":"result","duration_ms":39759,"uuid":"3a592d1c-67db-41b7-9433-5f669b9bb0ab","ttft_stream_ms":2519,"time_to_request_ms":645,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":33,"system_prompt":270,"resume_other":1,"process_user_inpu